EXT. FOXHOLE ABOVE A SUNKEN ROAD - DAWN

Frost on the parapet. The cold earth smells of wet iron. A field telephone sits on an ammunition crate, wires snaking away into the dark.

LIEUTENANT PIOTR WOZNIAK, thirties, hollow-cheeked, sits against the dirt wall with his knees drawn up and a tin of cold stew in his lap. He hasn't eaten any. His helmet is pushed back and his eyes are on the gray horizon.

The field telephone rings. Once, sharp, then silence, then again.

He picks up the receiver slowly, as though it might burn him.

WOZNIAK
Wozniak.

CAPTAIN HAVERFORD (V.O.)
(over the phone, crackling)
Piotr. It's Haverford. I've got your orders.

WOZNIAK
Go ahead, sir.

CAPTAIN HAVERFORD (V.O.)
Hold the road until noon. Then fall back to the ridge.

WOZNIAK
We have twelve men, sir.

CAPTAIN HAVERFORD (V.O.)
I know what you have.

Wozniak is silent. A distant mortar thuds. Soil trickles from the parapet.

WOZNIAK
Twelve men, and ammunition for an hour.

CAPTAIN HAVERFORD (V.O.)
Piotr. I'm sorry.

WOZNIAK
Sir, I'm not complaining.

CAPTAIN HAVERFORD (V.O.)
No. You never do.

He looks at the stew. Sets it aside.

WOZNIAK
Is there word from the north?

CAPTAIN HAVERFORD (V.O.)
Nothing. The line's been dead since midnight. You're my only link on this side.

WOZNIAK
Then I'll hold, sir.

A pause. The line hisses.

CAPTAIN HAVERFORD (V.O.)
Do you have a family, Lieutenant?

WOZNIAK
A sister. In Gdansk.

CAPTAIN HAVERFORD (V.O.)
Write to her. Today.

WOZNIAK
Yes, sir.

CAPTAIN HAVERFORD (V.O.)
Good man.

The line goes dead. Wozniak holds the receiver a long moment, then sets it gently back on its cradle.

He stands, brushes the frost from his sleeves, and looks across the sunken road at the first thin light.
